Root cause:
The `loadUserTypes` method was designed to iterate over a collection of user-defined types (UDTs), attempting to load each one and collecting failures into `notLoaded` for retry (fixed-point recursion). However, when the input `udts` collection was empty (i.e., a keyspace with no user types), the `for` loop body never executed, leaving `notLoaded` empty as well. The subsequent check `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` then evaluated `0 == 0`, which is true, causing the method to incorrectly throw a `RuntimeException("Unable to load user types [])` even though there was no actual failure—simply nothing to load. This edge case (zero UDTs) was not handled, causing the bulk loader to fail unnecessarily for keyspaces without any user-defined types.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`for (UserType ut : udts) { ... }`
`if (notLoaded.size() == udts.size()) { throw new RuntimeException("Unable to load user types " + notLoaded); }`
Reason: When `udts` is empty, the for loop body never executes, so `notLoaded` remains empty. The subsequent check `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` then compares `0 == 0`, which evaluates to true, incorrectly triggering the RuntimeException even though there was no actual failure—there was simply nothing to load.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`if (udts.isEmpty()) { return; }`
Reason: This explicitly handles the edge case of an empty `udts` collection by returning immediately before the loop and failure-check logic execute, preventing the erroneous `0 == 0` comparison from triggering the RuntimeException when there are no user types to load.


